EXAMPLES_2 = {
    "ch02-01-model-risks": """A churn-prediction model, three months live. Watch the seven risks introduce themselves by symptom. *Drift*: the market shifted and the model's accuracy decays — the data it sees now does not follow the patterns it learned, the spam-filter story. *Overfitting*: it was excellent on training data and immediately mediocre in the world. *Underfitting*: it was mediocre everywhere from day one, including the training set — the model was too simple to hold the pattern. *Leakage*: validation looked too good to be true, and an independent evaluator cannot reproduce the numbers — outside information was in the training set. *Bias*: right on average, skewed across groups. *Hallucination* (for a generative component): confident fabrications. *Transparency gap*: predictions are fine and nobody can say why. Each symptom is a different failure with a different fix — which is the whole point of holding the seven apart, and the reason [Ch. 02](../../chapters/02-evaluation-science-and-experimental-design/README.md) and [Ch. 17](../../chapters/17-distribution-shift-and-deployment-reliability/README.md) give them their own chapters.""",
    "ch02-02-common-risks-of-ai-models": """Five complaints, five diagnoses. *"Great in training, disappointing in production."* — the error lives on unseen data: overfitting. *"Poor everywhere, even on the data it trained on."* — underfitting; adding data will not fix a capacity problem. *"Validation accuracy nobody outside the team can reproduce."* — the evaluation was contaminated: data leakage. *"It was accurate last quarter and it is degrading now."* — the world moved: drift. *"Right on average, wrong for one group."* — bias. The exercise's stems are rotations of these five sentences with the industries swapped; the discrimination is always *where the error lives* — training set, held-out data, live traffic, or across groups. Locate the error first; the risk's name follows, and so does the fix.""",
    "ch02-03-continuous-monitoring": """The monitoring contract in one paragraph. *Baseline:* at launch, record the model's accuracy, its refusal rate, and its error rate per subgroup — taken on day zero, because a number without a pre-deployment referent has no meaning. *Thresholds:* decide in advance what change triggers action — a two-point accuracy drop, a doubling of any subgroup's error rate, a new class of complaint in the support queue. *Owner:* a named person receives the alert, not a channel. *Cadence:* weekly drift checks, monthly bias re-tests, quarterly full re-assessment — the video's early-warning system, the checks set from the formal assessment's results. Any answer that treats monitoring as a one-time check or a dashboard purchase is the marked-wrong one; monitoring is a cadence with thresholds and a named owner, or it is retention without watching.""",
    "ch02-04-model-risk-abyss": """One round, played slowly. The story: "Six months after launch, the fraud model's precision slipped from 91 to 84. The team retrained it twice; it did not help. Meanwhile a competitor launched a new product line that changed who shops when." First, cut the story noise (deadlines, opinions, the retraining attempts) and locate the error: it decayed over time in production — that is *drift*, and the retraining failed because the world changed again each time. The winning move in the game is closing the gap the title names: the abyss is the period between deployment and the next audit, so the fix is a baseline at launch, thresholds with an owner, and a re-check cadence — not another retrain. The game grades the diagnosis, and the diagnosis is the two-step: where does the error live, then which risk is it.""",
    "ch02-05-external-risks-in-ai": """One company, five attack surfaces. *Adversarial input:* a driver-assistance camera sees a modified stop sign and misclassifies it — the decision was manipulated at inference time. *Corruption and poisoning:* fake five-star reviews steer a recommender — the learning itself was corrupted at training time. *Theft and inversion:* researchers reconstruct training images from a facial-recognition model's outputs — the target is the training data, not the outputs and not the learning. *Infrastructure:* a flood of requests from many sources makes the service unavailable — the target is the hosting. *Supply chain:* a vulnerability planted in a widely used library ships inside every system built on it — the target is the development dependency. The taxonomy sorts by target and phase; learn the five targets and you can place any attack you read about, which is precisely how [Ch. 12](../../chapters/12-adversarial-ml-and-robustness/README.md) and [Ch. 16](../../chapters/16-privacy-provenance-and-supply-chains/README.md) organize their chapters.""",
    "ch02-06-types-of-external-risks": """Five stems, sorted on two axes. *"A sticker on a traffic sign makes the classifier read 85."* — inference-time manipulation of a decision: adversarial input. *"A vendor's review-import feed turns out to contain manufactured praise."* — training-time corruption of learning: poisoning. *"Querying the model lets an attacker extract verbatim training records."* — reconstruction of the training data: inversion (the theft-and-inversion group, aimed at the data). *"The endpoint is overwhelmed by coordinated requests."* — the target is hosting: infrastructure. *"A popular dependency is found to have a backdoor."* — the target is the component: supply chain. The two confusions the exercise plants are the phase pair (poisoning versus adversarial — training versus inference) and the data pair (inversion reconstructs the data; theft copies the model). Sort by target and phase, and both confusions dissolve.""",
    "ch02-07-keeping-up-with-external-ai-risks": """Map each practice to the group it counters. *Regular security audits* → surface vulnerabilities across groups. *Data validation and sanitization* → guard the integrity of training and inference data against poisoning and crafted inputs. *Strong access controls* → prevent unauthorized use: model theft, breaches. *Encryption and anonymization* → protect sensitive information even if it leaks: inversion, exposure. *Continuous monitoring* → catch emerging threats as they develop. *Trusted, regularly updated development tools* → secure the supply chain at the dependency level. *Ongoing education and training* → the human layer every group exploits first. The rule the exercise encodes: defense is layered because attacks are phased — no single practice covers two groups, and any answer claiming one practice closes the whole surface is the distractor.""",
    "ch02-08-perimeter-peril-point": """The incident: "Order recommendations across the platform turned strange overnight — the system began pushing a product no genuine user had rated." The triage, under pressure: classify first, then counter by group. The fault entered through the training data (the interaction stream was seeded), which makes it **poisoning** — so the response is on the data side: freeze the suspect feed, audit provenance, retrain from a verified baseline. Responding with input filters would be the misread: filters guard inference, and this attack never touched inference. The game's losing moves are always phase misreads — retraining for a flood, filtering for a poisoning, absorbing an adversarial input with more capacity. The peril in the title is exactly that misread, priced immediately.""",
    "ch02-09-secure-development-practices": """A loan-approval model's data pipeline, walked through the four practices in order. *Provenance and traceability:* every field carries its origin — which bureau, which upload, which consent — so when poisoned or corrupt data is found, it can be pinpointed and removed. *Validation:* at ingestion, the data is checked and cleaned — accurate, relevant, untampered — before it ever reaches training. *Minimization:* the pipeline asks of every field "do we need this?" and drops what it does not — every uncollected datum is a breach that cannot happen. *Encryption and anonymization:* what remains is transformed for storage and stripped of identifiers where possible — the last line against model inversion. Then the non-data practices around it: secure coding (reviews, scanning, standards), access management (only trusted, verified parties may modify the model or its data), and secure infrastructure (firewalls, intrusion detection, secure cloud). The video's closing move is the beginner's headline: security is built by the teams that create the system, and it is not the tech team's job alone.""",
    "ch02-10-secure-ai-development": """Four needs, four practices, matched by purpose. *"Where did this data come from, and what happened to it?"* — provenance and traceability; the anti-poisoning record. *"Is this data accurate and untampered before training?"* — validation; the pre-ingestion gate. *"Do we need this much data at all?"* — minimization; the breach-surface reducer. *"What happens if the store leaks anyway?"* — encryption and anonymization; the last line. The distractor pairings are the graded content: minimization does not detect tampering; validation does not remove identifiers; encryption does not tell you the data's origin. If the purpose sentence does not follow from the practice's mechanism, reject the pairing — that is the whole exercise, and the whole discipline.""",
    "ch02-11-secure-access": """One question, two sides. *Who may read?* — analysts, auditors, the monitoring system: read access follows role. *Who may modify?* — the model, its weights, its training data: write access follows verification, and this is the security-critical side, because every external attack group that targets learning or data is looking for a write path. The exercise's stems grant access because someone asked, is in a hurry, or is senior — each is the marked-wrong answer. The principle in one line, the video's own: only trusted, verified individuals or entities may modify the model or its data; authorization follows verification, never convenience. [Ch. 14](../../chapters/14-agent-tool-memory-mcp-security/README.md) is where the same rule meets AI agents that hold tools.""",
    "ch02-12-fortified-code-forge": """The forge hands you a budget and a threat profile. Say the profile is: a recommender fed by user-generated content (poisoning risk high), a public API (adversarial inputs possible), and a small trust team (insider risk low, fatigue risk high). The winning allocation: provenance on the content feed first (cheapest effective counter to the highest-probability group), input validation at the API second, minimization on the profile store third (the data is sensitive), and access control on the training pipeline fourth. What the game grades is the *sequencing under constraint* — severity times likelihood under budget — which is chapter 3's threat prioritization arriving early as play. The forge's lesson: security is built, not bought, and the right four bars go on the door that is actually under attack.""",
    "ch02-13-ai-risk-assessment-frameworks": """One system, through both prongs. *The system:* an AI tool that screens job applications. *The formal prong:* identify the risks (bias in decisions, privacy in the data, opacity in the reasoning); analyze likelihood and impact; evaluate against the organization's tolerance; strategize — and classify the tier: screening job applicants sits in the EU AI Act's high-risk band, so a documented risk assessment and risk management system are required, not optional. *The continuous prong:* from the assessment's results, set the ongoing checks — regular bias testing on the live decisions, monitoring for unexpected behavior, drift watching — the early-warning system that catches what the formal pass could not see. The video's closing rule is the beginner's headline: this is not ticking boxes; it is a culture of continuous vigilance supported by formal controls, and neither prong works alone.""",
    "ch02-14-the-eu-ai-act": """Four systems, four tiers. *A social-scoring system ranking citizens* — unacceptable: banned. *An AI screener for job applications* — high risk: a documented risk assessment and risk management system are required. *A chatbot that must identify itself as AI* — limited risk: transparency duties. *A spam filter* — minimal risk: voluntary codes of conduct suffice. The exercise's trap is capability language: stems describing what a system *can* do are bait, because the tier follows the *use* and the riskiness of the deployment context, never the model's cleverness or size. A powerful model in a minimal-risk use is minimal risk; a modest model in a high-risk use is high risk. The Act's own logic, as the video states it: risk assessment at the cornerstone of regulating development AND deployment.""",
    "ch02-15-risk-assessment-steps": """The four steps, with the right output at each. *Identify:* the list — privacy issues, biases, security vulnerabilities, named one by one. *Analyze:* likelihood and impact per risk, each with its evidence. *Evaluate:* the ranking, against seriousness and the organization's tolerance — this is where the prioritization is born. *Strategize:* the plan — manage, reduce, or eliminate, with owners and dates. The exercise grades the ORDER and the output type per step, and the classic wrong answers are outputs arriving early: a strategy at the analyze step (spending before ranking), an evaluation with no likelihoods (a ranking with nothing under it). Why order matters: each step's output is the next step's input, and the video's two-pronged approach requires the sequence to have run once, correctly, before anything downstream can be set from its results.""",
    "ch02-16-assessment-framework-enclave": """Design the assessment for a fictional insurer using AI claims triage. *Tier it first:* triaging insurance claims touches financial consequences for individuals — high-risk band, so the formal prong is mandatory. *Run the four steps with stakeholders in the room:* the adjusters, legal, the data science team, and an executive sponsor — the diverse perspectives the video requires for a comprehensive view. *Set the continuous checks from the results:* bias testing across claim types on a cadence, monitoring for drift in claim patterns, and a named owner for each threshold. *State the culture posture:* vigilance, not box-ticking — the enclave's output is trust, and trust is the thing being constructed. The game scores one of these per round; the full run is the chapter played end to end, and the repo's [Ch. 22](../../chapters/22-safety-engineering-and-assurance/README.md) is where each round gets its formal treatment.""",
}
